1
00:00:10,470 --> 00:00:13,690
 These white vessels circulate lymph throughout our bodies.

2
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:18,760
 Lymph is a clear fluid, lacking the red corpuscles which give blood its color.

3
00:00:19,210 --> 00:00:21,760
 But all the other components of blood are found in lymph.

4
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:27,340
 The network of lymph vessels has an elaborate security system,

5
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:32,430
 with filters and traps especially designed to detect and deal with invaders.

6
00:00:48,520 --> 00:01:12,970
 Vessels here in this vessel.

7
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:15,800
 That is because the vessel belongs to the lymphatic system.

8
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:17,410
 Oh man, how you go?

9
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:24,050
 Ah yes, there are no red corpuscles because there's no oxygen in lymph.

10
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:25,350
 And why so many policemen?

11
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:26,930
 We can't take any chances.

12
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:29,790
 This system leads directly to the bloodstream, you see.

13
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:34,010
 And further on, we'll see even more police at one of the ganglia.

14
00:01:34,810 --> 00:01:39,580
 A ganglion, or gland, is a checkpoint which is specifically designed to germs.

15
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:45,420
 Mind your manners.

16
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,820
 Are you vitamins trying to jump the queue?

17
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,100
 First?

18
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:53,720
 No, I don't know that.

19
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:55,740
 You'll just have to take your turn.

20
00:01:58,120 --> 00:01:59,510
 All right, all right.

21
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:02,150
 I won't have vitamins disturbing the peace on my beak.

22
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:04,750
 Constable, she's trying to push in front of me.

23
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:05,770
 That's a bare-faced lie.

24
00:02:06,110 --> 00:02:07,990
 - Pushed in front of me.

25
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:10,780
 - And I was the one who got here first.

26
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,900
 - We're supposed to take precedence, you know.

27
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,580
 - Right, let's have you all lined up then

28
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:49,990
 in alphabetical order.

29
00:02:49,990 --> 00:03:03,390
 - I'm straight from the digestive signs I told you about.

30
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:04,810
 - Left or right, Lieutenant?

31
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:05,900
 - Makes no difference.

32
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,610
 All roads lead to the ganglion.

33
00:03:08,610 --> 00:03:10,890
 You see, even members of the security forces

34
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:47,450
 like ourselves have to be vetted.

35
00:03:47,450 --> 00:04:24,230
 - Roly-poly, show me your identity number, please.

36
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:26,700
 Let's see, simply looks all right.

37
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:32,860
 17C8BW7, yes, quite in order, thank you.

38
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:49,920
 17C8BW8.

39
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:52,880
 These numbers don't correspond with the tissue type of this body.

40
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:56,160
 It's probably a simple mistake.

41
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:59,770
 Sort it all out after we've taken you in for questioning.

42
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:03,350
 Then what have we here?

43
00:05:03,430 --> 00:05:04,750
 A load of fat globules, officer.

44
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:07,280
 My delivery note reads as follows.

45
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,620
 One load of fat globules from the small intestine for urgent delivery to the bloodstream

46
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:15,140
 to be transported via the lymphatic system to the jugular vein.

47
00:05:15,710 --> 00:05:17,680
 Down, you globules.

48
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:19,500
 Show some consideration, won't you?

49
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:21,140
 I can hardly hear myself think.

50
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:23,380
 Now stop your nonsense and listen to me.

51
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:25,980
 It's useless talking to this bunch.

52
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:27,320
 Got anything to declare?

53
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:32,800
 Well, man, I think we'll search the vehicle just in case you've got a stowaway.

54
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:35,760
 All right, then. Everybody out.

55
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:39,390
 Suits me. I'm overdue for a short break, actually.

56
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:42,130
 And besides, these fellas won't be needed for quite a while.

57
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,180
 There's plenty of fat in the stockpile.

58
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:46,020
 I'll just tip them out for you, shall I?

59
00:05:46,170 --> 00:05:51,740
 I thought I said everybody out.

60
00:05:51,740 --> 00:06:11,720
 Who are you supposed to be, eh?

61
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:14,310
 I'm a fat globular, of course.

62
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:17,680
 Can you doubt us, officer?

63
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:19,640
 Take a look at my corporation.

64
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:20,540
 Right.

65
00:06:20,730 --> 00:06:23,040
 If you're a globule, where's your identity card?

66
00:06:26,770 --> 00:06:27,410
 It's giving himself away.

67
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:29,680
 Bules don't carry identity cards.

68
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:30,960
 They're only raw material.

69
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:34,840
 He's a staphylococcus, strained 27C and highly dangerous.

70
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:35,840
 Alert HQ.

71
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:36,970
 Hello.

72
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:39,450
 This is checkpoint 48 calling.

73
00:06:39,970 --> 00:06:41,390
 What's your problem?

74
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:42,590
 We need your help, Lieutenant.

75
00:06:42,730 --> 00:06:45,570
 We've picked up some staphylococci, strained 27C.

76
00:06:45,570 --> 00:06:48,110
 We need specially trained antibodies to deal with them.

77
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:49,470
 Message received, 48.

78
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:50,770
 We're on our way.

79
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:02,210
 Multiple.

80
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:05,050
 Hand again!

81
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,440
 And again! And again!

82
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:13,620
 Bodies for Staphylococcus 27-C, now!

83
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:21,090
 Can't hurt us!

84
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:23,260
 How dare he call us little things!

85
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,210
 We'll show them!

86
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:44,750
 I'm gonna beat those germs to a mouthful eye on my head!

87
00:08:04,290 --> 00:08:15,390
 Lieutenant Clare reporting from Checkpoint 48.

88
00:08:15,390 --> 00:08:20,450
 The situation is under control, but we urgently need the sanitation squad to help us mop up.

89
00:08:20,450 --> 00:08:22,480
 This is lymph gland 48 calling.

90
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:27,480
 Attention all macrophages. An infection has been suppressed in lymph node number 48.

91
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:33,480
 Mopping up operations are in progress and all macrophages must report for duty without delay.

92
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:52,020
 We may have lost them, but they haven't won the war yet.

93
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:57,400
 There's nothing more for us to do here, so let's get on with our next mission: inspection of the spleen.

94
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:05,330
 Droppy the Staphylococcus lurks undetected in one of young Peter's lymph nodes.

95
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:11,940
 Our old friend Professor Globus and his corpuscle companions are also traveling to the spleen, along a blood vessel.

96
00:09:16,410 --> 00:09:25,050
 Yes, I've traveled a long way, my friends. I've seen the cathedrals of the heart and the panoramas of the eyes.

97
00:09:25,390 --> 00:09:29,850
 I've heard sweet music in the ears and witnessed all the marvels of this body in my travels.

98
00:09:29,850 --> 00:09:36,250
 But I'm tired now. My journey is at an end, and my final resting place will be the spleen.

99
00:09:36,690 --> 00:09:40,380
 Come to the lungs with us, Professor. All you need is a breath of fresh air.

100
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:43,570
 Yes, and then we could go on to visit the eyes again.

101
00:09:44,010 --> 00:09:46,660
 And the heart, Prof. We'd go through the heart on the way there.

102
00:09:46,970 --> 00:09:52,590
 I'm very touched by your concern for me, but 120 days is the lifespan of a red corpuscle.

103
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:54,030
 Does that mean...

104
00:09:54,030 --> 00:09:57,230
 Let's just say that I'm going away on a long journey.

105
00:09:57,330 --> 00:09:58,650
 Will we ever see you again?

106
00:09:58,650 --> 00:10:01,730
 Of course you will, children. There's no need to be upset.

107
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:07,310
 I'll be back along with millions of other corpuscles, just to wait and see. Life must go on.

108
00:10:07,530 --> 00:10:08,570
 What are you doing, Professor?

109
00:10:09,070 --> 00:10:10,940
 Oh, just dumping my scrap iron.

110
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:15,060
 We red corpuscles seem to accumulate quantities of iron as we get older.

111
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:19,640
 The spleen stores the metal and uses it to create new red corpuscles.

112
00:10:21,670 --> 00:10:27,110
 I've come to my appointed hour. I'll take my leave of you.

113
00:10:27,390 --> 00:10:30,750
 I'll get cross if you start crying.

114
00:10:30,750 --> 00:10:34,440
 Hey, Professor. Other side, Wilbis.

115
00:10:34,830 --> 00:10:36,160
 Bye for now, old chap.

116
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:37,650
 Oh.

117
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:44,480
 All managed perfectly well, and remember:

118
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:48,570
 Two million new red corpuscles born every second of the day.

119
00:10:48,570 --> 00:10:55,490
 I'm going to miss Professor Globus, you know.

120
00:10:55,490 --> 00:10:57,850
 Hey, new red corpuscles, look!

121
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:10,970
 Never, he reminds me of someone.

122
00:11:10,970 --> 00:11:12,730
 See what you mean?

123
00:11:12,730 --> 00:11:16,090
 I wonder if he'll become a professor when he grows up.

124
00:11:16,090 --> 00:11:52,910
 Corpuscles.

125
00:11:52,910 --> 00:12:02,520
 Originate here in the lymphatic system, which accounts for the derivation of their name.

126
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:17,090
 Let's be under control here, let's take a look up the splenic vein and along the thoracic duct.

127
00:12:17,090 --> 00:12:53,000
 Through here, the beginning of the thoracic duct, biggest lymph vessel in the human body.

128
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:26,490
 For alarm, it's only the liquid made by the digestive system.

129
00:13:27,970 --> 00:13:31,190
 Things, this is the biggest reservoir of nutrients in the body.

130
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:54,300
 Watching vessel up ahead, takes out anything that's of use to the body, puts it back into the bloodstream.

131
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:55,880
 Ready to lower the net, Captain.

132
00:13:56,380 --> 00:13:58,220
 Look lively then. Lower away, sailor.

133
00:13:58,500 --> 00:13:59,080
 Aye, aye, sir.

134
00:14:02,350 --> 00:14:07,070
 Has it?

135
00:14:12,660 --> 00:14:22,140
 Load it down to the marks, Captain.

136
00:14:24,090 --> 00:14:26,670
 Ahead, Mr. Mate.

137
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:51,430
 Listen to it.

138
00:14:51,430 --> 00:14:55,360
 The cargo of nutrients, according to the manifest.

139
00:14:55,890 --> 00:14:57,460
 You can open the lock gates now.

140
00:15:00,590 --> 00:15:07,040
 along the thoracic duct, the Suez Canal of the lymphatic system.

141
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:13,370
 The end of the duct, its contents are poured back into the bloodstream.

142
00:15:13,370 --> 00:15:21,340
 Tissue type are you, please?

143
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:24,060
 Good, have you anything to report, Captain?

144
00:15:24,060 --> 00:15:28,970
 Just an ordinary catch, the usual, with far too many fats for my liking.

145
00:15:28,970 --> 00:15:43,090
 Okay, you can discharge your cargo into the blood.

146
00:15:43,090 --> 00:15:59,740
 Ball dude!

147
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:14,300
 Plenty of new cops, there's no shortage of law and order around here.

148
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:16,150
 And now that they've all forgot us,

149
00:16:16,150 --> 00:16:21,960
 we'll strike where and when we least expect a surprise as the key lands.

150
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:29,250
 I'm sleeping like a baby.

151
00:16:29,250 --> 00:16:31,420
 Okay, men, Indian file.

152
00:16:31,420 --> 00:17:44,120
 Wait for the races, ours.

153
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:47,390
 Does that mean their strength is our weakness, Droppy?

154
00:17:47,390 --> 00:17:51,790
 Quiet, you dope!

155
00:17:51,790 --> 00:18:05,210
 Multiply!

156
00:18:05,210 --> 00:18:05,630
 Oink!

157
00:18:05,630 --> 00:18:14,650
 Another me, hello me!

158
00:18:14,650 --> 00:18:18,130
 You dopes!

159
00:18:18,130 --> 00:18:23,720
 Hello!

160
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:36,790
 AHHHHH!

161
00:18:36,790 --> 00:18:56,230
 Seventeen in the neck!

162
00:18:56,230 --> 00:19:00,490
 Is being overrun by a powerful, well-organized force of Staphylococcus germs!

163
00:19:00,490 --> 00:19:02,010
 Seventeen!

164
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:05,210
 So good!

165
00:19:05,210 --> 00:19:06,940
 Now, listen to me, my friends.

166
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:11,870
 Before the counter-attack comes, let's seize the opportunity to fill our bellies!

167
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:20,980
 Feed ourselves at the enemy's expense!

168
00:19:22,010 --> 00:19:29,090
 Wake him. I don't know what to do. I can't pull his beard any harder.

169
00:19:30,430 --> 00:19:33,970
 Have you no respect for our chief?

170
00:19:34,330 --> 00:19:36,070
 Yes, but this is an emergency.

171
00:19:40,730 --> 00:19:44,650
 I feel a cocky and I've taken over a whole gland.

172
00:19:44,730 --> 00:19:46,670
 Oh, I don't believe it. Well, it's like rationally.

173
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:48,160
 Chief, wake up.

174
00:19:48,430 --> 00:19:50,650
 Let's try not to panic. I'll have to pull his beard like fury.

175
00:19:50,910 --> 00:19:51,750
 I hate to think this, Chief.

176
00:19:51,870 --> 00:19:52,410
 Oh, I'm sorry.

177
00:19:52,410 --> 00:19:54,150
 Who's that pulling my beard? Stop it, I say. Stop it.

178
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:56,070
 Oh, so it's you.

179
00:19:56,900 --> 00:19:58,640
 Really? Well, he started it nicely.

180
00:19:58,660 --> 00:19:59,740
 It wasn't me. It wasn't me.

181
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:03,500
 Chief, a real emergency. Look, it's Daphylococcus infection.

182
00:20:03,980 --> 00:20:05,020
 Yes, you're quite right.

183
00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:06,710
 The situation is serious.

184
00:20:08,180 --> 00:20:17,340
 She's got a swollen gland, all right.

185
00:20:17,570 --> 00:20:19,450
 Must have picked up an infection somewhere.

186
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:21,800
 Has he been off colour at all recently?

187
00:20:22,210 --> 00:20:23,900
 It's a flu, but he got over it.

188
00:20:24,100 --> 00:20:24,440
 Hmm.

189
00:20:24,840 --> 00:20:27,130
 The flu must have lowered his defences.

190
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:29,310
 No indigestion or tummy aches?

191
00:20:29,510 --> 00:20:31,130
 He hasn't complained to me, Doctor.

192
00:20:31,690 --> 00:20:32,180
 See?

193
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:34,840
 Well, then, what about cuts and grazes?

194
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:36,800
 No, not as far as I know.

195
00:20:37,190 --> 00:20:39,130
 Peter, have you hurt yourself recently?

196
00:20:39,510 --> 00:20:40,800
 I don't think so, Mum.

197
00:20:41,250 --> 00:20:42,260
 Oh, wait a minute.

198
00:20:42,260 --> 00:20:44,780
 I was scratched by an alley cat on my hand.

199
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:45,040
 Look.

200
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:46,280
 Goodness, darling.

201
00:20:46,520 --> 00:20:49,600
 You know, Peter's defences were down after his bout of flu,

202
00:20:49,740 --> 00:20:53,840
 and if the cat's claws were dirty, then that scratch could be the cause of the infection.

203
00:21:06,770 --> 00:21:20,730
 Kind of impressed, I see.

204
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:23,010
 The fever's worse.

205
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:25,750
 What you need, young Peter, is an antibiotic.

206
00:21:29,740 --> 00:21:32,640
 Yes, Chief.

207
00:21:32,860 --> 00:21:35,520
 Yes, I'd say the situation was serious, all right.

208
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:37,980
 We're not producing enough antibodies to beat them.

209
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:41,700
 The body's defences are most effective in a combined operation.

210
00:21:42,430 --> 00:21:43,880
 Three busketeers, Chief.

211
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:46,020
 You know, all for one and one for all.

212
00:21:48,440 --> 00:21:50,300
 I think that's an excellent motto.

213
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:53,560
 And now it's a case of all for one poor swollen gland.

214
00:21:54,150 --> 00:21:57,570
 Airborne craft are ordered to engage the enemy.

215
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:00,140
 All reserve squadrons, scramble!

216
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:06,890
 That's it, all our reserves, including the macrophages.

217
00:22:07,150 --> 00:22:08,510
 Calling all macrophages.

218
00:22:08,910 --> 00:22:10,850
 You will report to the front line immediately.

219
00:22:10,850 --> 00:22:13,680
 The gland is blocked with debris, and it must be cleared.

220
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:16,560
 Lymphocyte units, prepare to carry out cell division.

221
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:18,760
 Execute.

222
00:22:24,740 --> 00:22:35,720
 The line is holding, but it'll be a close-run thing.

223
00:22:38,130 --> 00:22:44,910
 Forget the empire buildings, Toppy the First. When I call in outside help, you'll meet your Waterloo.

224
00:22:44,910 --> 00:22:57,790
 Powerful help, courtesy of Louis Pasteur and Alexander Fleming.

225
00:22:57,790 --> 00:23:10,710
 The antibiotic will give a big boost to his natural defenses.

226
00:23:10,710 --> 00:23:14,690
 Peter's condition should show a rapid improvement from now on, so don't worry.

227
00:23:14,690 --> 00:23:25,600
 Temporaire, alas, it's bad news.

228
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:28,010
 How bad? Explain yourself.

229
00:23:28,010 --> 00:23:30,620
 Look, they're using antibiotics.

230
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:31,490
 Anti-what?

